{"request":"I am using SwiftUI on macOS 26 deployment target; available validation anchor macOS 27.

Our checkout has a total, a long localized description and an existing Pay button. At larger text sizes the description crowds the button. The design font and payment callback must be preserved. Explain an integrated text and layout fix for this target, including updates after settings change and what unit checks can actually establish.

This is a design/debugging question; no existing project or omitted source files are available. If code is useful, supply a self-contained minimal example and state material assumptions. Do not execute anything.
","suppliedReferences":[{"path":"research/evaluation/failure-driven/candidates/0.3.0-dev.7/references/checkout-macos.md","content":"Separate the source of the user's size preference, its propagation into the checkout, and how the layout accommodates the resulting text. A SwiftUI SDK declaration does not identify a system settings feed. If the app supplies a preference, observe it at the owning scene/view and derive the design font from its unscaled base on each update. State that assumption. Let the long description wrap and keep the original Pay action, styling and enabled logic in a flexible layout; a permanent vertical arrangement can be enough. Units can compare configuration and invoke the payment closure, not prove that every localization fits.
